  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\n We address the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by developing a multiresolution optimization approach designed to systematically explore solution spaces of increasing resolution. The core idea is to start with a known solution and iteratively refine it at higher resolutions, leveraging the structure of the problem to find improved candidates. This approach differs from the initial program's multistart local optimization by incorporating:\n 1) Resolution-guided sampling instead of uniform perturbation\n 2) Constraint-aware initialization via parent solution interpolation\n 3) Dynamic basis construction for smooth function approximation\n 4) Multi-resolution optimization scheduled by time budget\n\n The algorithm targets the objective C_5 by minimizing the maximum cross-correlation between h and its complement. Constraints are strictly enforced. We project parent solutions to higher resolutions while preserving the core pattern, then apply local search with informed sampling. The method systematically pushes the solution toward the theoretical lower bound by resolving oscillations and discontinuities.\n\n Mathematical specification\n We represent h as a step function h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] with discrete approximation of size n_points. The optimization objective is:\n C_5 = max_{k \\in \\mathbb{Z}} \\frac{1}{n_{\\text{points}}} \\cdot \\max_{\\text{mode='full'}} \\text{np.correlate}(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")\n\n Parameters:\n \u2022 n_points: number of samples (starting at 380 for initial solution)\n \u2022 dx = 2.0 / n_points (spatial resolution)\n \u2022 lag_range = list of shift distances for correlation (negative to positive lags)\n\n Constraints manipulation:\n  - [0,1] clamping via clip_then_rescale \n  - Sum enforcement via water-filling projection\n\n We formulate the optimization problem with:\n  \u2022 Objective function: max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx \n  \u2022 Variables: h_i \u2208 [0,1] for all i\n  \u2022 Equality constraint: \u03a3h_i / n_{\\text{points}} = 0.5\n\n We use projected gradient optimization (CG with function barzilar) with:\n  \u2022 Line search using backtracking\n  \u2022 Constraint restoration if violated\n\n Implementation procedure\n 1. Initialization\n    \u2022 Start with parent solution interpolation (reweight basis functions first)\n    \u2022 Generate samples from low-resolution candidates\n    \u2022 Local expansion radially to create a candidate set matching the parent function\n\n 2. Optimization loop\n    \u2022 For each resolution, scale parent solution to current points\n    \u2022 Apply projection to satisfy constraints\n    \u2022 Perform projected gradient descent with automatic basis adaptation\n    \u2022 Maintain a population archive of improving candidates\n    \u2022 Track wall time and adjust resolution schedule\n\n 3. Candidate acceptance criteria\n    \u2022 Update best solution if C_5 is improved (e.g., lower value)\n    \u2022 Terminate early if C_5 < 0.3808\n\n 4. Fallback procedure\n    \u2022 If multiple resolutions available, use best from closest resolution\n    \u2022 Otherwise depend purely on parent solution\n\n Search choices and useful variations\n  \u2022 We use a multirate decomposition (starting low then high resolution)\n  \u2022 Time allocations: \n    - n_points=380 (10%), n_points=760 (40%), n_points=3040 (50%)\n  \u2022 Optimization steps per resolution: 1000-2000 iterations\n\n Numerical robustness\n  \u2022 Impose bounds and constraints at every step\n  \u2022 Fail-safe fallback to parent solution if optimization fails\n  \u2022 Use relative tolerance parameters (rtol=1e-6)\n\n Runtime safeguards\n  \u2022 Measure elapsed time across resolutions\n  \u2022 Maintain best solution so far (last resort fallback)\n  \u2022 Budget allocation: \n    - Resolution expansion: 30% for whole-loop time\n    - Optimization: 70% per resolution\n\n  Return contract\n    returns (best_h, C_5_value, n_final)\n  Constraint validation\n    - Explicit bounds and sum enforcement at every optimization step\n    - Final projection ensures domain satisfaction\n    - Feasibility check before scoring",
